<title>BELGIUM: Commission backs Irish cut-price butter.</title>
<headline>Commission backs Irish cut-price butter.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-12-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission has backed an Irish government request to be allowed to sell butter to people on social assistance at reduced prices, an EU official said on Monday.</p>
<p>The proposal which must be agreed by the Agriculture Council, renews a scheme (Reg EEC No 2990/82 and 82/3) which will expire on December 31.</p>
<p>The amended proposal would give the Irish government an additional two years to sell cheap butter to those on social assistance.</p>
<p>Ireland is the only country to operate the scheme since it was introduced in 1978. Among the beneficiaries are pensioners, those drawing widows and orphans allowances, deserted wives and the blind.</p>
</text>
